            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

AIR SEALING



Overall, your house is moderately leaky from an air leakage perspective, and I have identified a few locations that can be addressed:



** The largest source is the open combustion air pipe (5" in diameter) located beside the furnace in your mechanical room on the ground floor - this pipe is likely a significant cause of this room being cold. With a furnace upgrade to a new condensing furnace (propane or oil) it would eliminate the combustion air pipe.



** The second largest air leakage source identified is through the mechanical room wall, likely through the header spaces, and around the sill plate (sitting on top of the concrete foundation). A typical wall is closed off to the outdoors, reducing air leakage, but the wall in this room has been built on the inside and does not seal well at the top, bottom, or sides, meaning there's room for air to leak in through the headers, and around the wall.



** Some windows are leaky around the frames, or do not latch well, and shoud be addressed with a full frame window replacement. See Window upgrade section for more details.



** Around the attic hatch is a small source of air leakage upstairs. This can be sealed with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack".



** The main bathroom exhaust fan on the upper floor was a bit leaky when the home was depressurized. Bathroom fans can be replaced with energy star rated fans with butterfly style dampers, that seal better than traditional exhaust fan dampers.



** Around the outside edge of the back exterior door, opening onto the deck. This door is newer, but consider replacing weather stripping as needed, and ensure the latches are working as intended.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss, but it could result in a larger reduction. This is estimated to save you $240 per year and decrease your annual energy usage by 7.0 GJ. Although the savings aren't huge, the increase in comfort downstairs will be noticeable, for relatively low effort/ expense.



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

WALL INSULATION



MAIN WALLS



The main exterior walls of your home, for the purposes of our modelling, were divided up into three types. The first type is what could be considered as the main walls, which are present around the outside exterior of the home. They appear to be 2x6 construction with batt insulation (R20). The other main wall type observed, is the walls that share a border with your garage, which is a space that is only heated to a low temperature, and these walls appear to be of 2x4 construction with R12 batts in the cavities. The final wall type observed is only present in the mechanical room and is basically a 2x6 wall, with an additional insulated wall constructed on the inside (~R30). Since you mentioned that you plan to complete a siding upgrade on your home, I have modelled adding 2 additional inches (R10) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to every exterior surface, which would include the inside portion of the garage walls. Due to the large amount of labour involved in re-siding a home, and the high cost, this recommendation should only be taken in conjunction with a siding upgrade.



Savings: By adding 2 inches (R10) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 740 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 22 GJ, and qualify for up to a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.



ARCTIC ENERGY ALLIANCE - DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM



If you choose to insulate the exterior walls of your home with a minimum of R10 effective, you may qualify for AEA's Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. By upgrading your exterior walls, you unlock the potential for more rebate dollars, please see 'Additional Comments' for more details, and BE SURE TO CALL ME BEFORE YOU BEGIN YOUR INSULATION UPGRADE, to ensure funding is available for you from this program. Funding is available on a first come, first served basis.

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

WINDOWS



The windows in your house consist mostly of older, triple paned, wood framed windows, with no low e coatings. Upgrading all 10 windows in this house to ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3 is something that could be considered as an upgrade for this home. If this is something the homeowner wishes to pursue I recommend full frame replacement windows to vinyl frames that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing all the older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $130 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.8 GJ. 



There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, however there are rebates available from the Greener Homes Grant - Government of Canada. Please see their website for more details on program requirements.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</windows>
                <doors>

DOOR UPGRADE



The doors in your home are of various ages and styles, and considered to be in good condition, and the savings on new doors will be minimal. Weather stripping was noted to be in reasonable shape at most locations, but could be repaired around the door leading onto the back deck. The door leading to your garage, from the ground floor was discovered to be a solid wood door, with a very low R value, and your garage is heated to a low temperature, so I've modelled replacing this door as an upgrade for your home. By replacing the solid wood door, with an energy stat rated door, you could save up to $80 per year in energy costs and reduce your energy consumption by 2.3 GJ/yr.



There are currenlty no rebates available from the GNWT for door replacements.



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $125 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</doors>
                <HeatingSystem>

FURNACE UPGRADE



One of the largest sources of cold air and discomfort found in your home was the combustion air supply pipe that is open, and constantly dumping cold, outside air into your mechanical room during the heating season. This is a typical installation style for older furnaces, but by upgrading to a direct vent furnace, the combustion air, never interacts with air in the home. It eliminates that cold air source.



PROPANE FURNACE



Your house is currently heated with an 83.4% efficient, oil furnace. I have modeled the following furnace upgrade option for you:



An ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



This upgrade is currently eligible for a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Full program details are outlined on our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program. This upgrade will eliminate the need for the current chimney and the 5" open combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room, and instead can be side wall vented, similar to your on demand hot water tank. 



Savings:



By replacing your current propane furnace with a 96% AFUE condensing propane furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $320 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 34 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



*** DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM ***



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify you for the maximum $20,000 rebate from this program. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP that, together, will be capped at $20,000.



     - up to $8,000 for the wall insulation - minimum 20% of the exterior wall upgrading by R10

     - up to $3,000 for the windows  - $250 per window, max 12 windows

     - up to $1,500 for the attic insulation

     - up to $3,000 for the foundation insulation - $250 per window, max 12 windows

     - $1,500 for the heating system

     - up to $1,000 for the air leakage reduction

     - up to $4,000 for the bonus rebate



In addition to the $20,000 rebate from the DHERP, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:



     - ESTAR fridge &amp; washing machine - $200 each

     - ESTAR ventless (condensing) clothes dryer - $400

     - ECM Pumps (for your boiler &amp; the water circulation pump) - $175 each - 2 per fiscal year

     - Programmable Thermostats - 50% up to $150 each - 2 per fiscal year

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.2 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace vents up a chimney and is susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

         

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife



The following December 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.25 per liter 

Propane - $1.00 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.99 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



